---
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';
import sources from '../data/sources.json';
import provenance from '../data/provenance.json';
// Generated from the method record by scripts/sync-method.ts.
const referenceList: { url: string; label: string; kind: string }[] = sources;
---
<Shell title="Sources & methodology" active="sources"><article class="prose"><span class="eyebrow">Follow the reference</span><h1>Sources & methodology</h1> <p>This page lists the sources behind the zakat method used by Zakat Calculator. The method itself is shown in full on the <a href="/method/">method page</a>.</p><h2>Current state</h2> <p><strong>Source-checked, not scholar-reviewed.</strong> Every Quran text, hadith reference, nisab conversion and position comes from a method record whose citations were opened and checked a second time. No scholar has reviewed it, and the calculator is not a fatwa.</p><h2>What the method page shows</h2> <ul><li>The textual basis for the obligation, the recipients, the rate and the lunar-year condition: Quran in the Pickthall translation, hadith by collection, number and grading as sunnah.com shows it.</li><li>Both nisab bases: the textual weights and the published modern conversions, each attributed. The calculator does not choose one for you.</li><li>For each point where scholars differ, every labelled option, the school or body it is attributed to, and the page that states it. A position that could not be checked is left out and noted.</li></ul><h2>What is editorial</h2> <p>Interface wording, labels and the explanations of how the calculation works are Moongazer's editorial text. They are not a ruling from any scholar or body. The calculator is not a fatwa.</p><h2>References used in this app</h2> {referenceList.length === 0 ? <p>None.</p> : <ul class="source-list">{referenceList.map(source=><><li><a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.label} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> <small>{source.kind}</small></li>{' '}</>)}</ul>}<h2>Snapshot and reuse</h2> <p>Original application code is MIT licensed. DM Sans, Newsreader and Amiri are hosted locally under the SIL Open Font License, with their licence files included.</p><p class="micro">Status: <code>{provenance.status}</code><br />App content SHA-256: <code style="overflow-wrap:anywhere">{provenance.appContentSha256}</code></p><div class="actions top-gap"><a class="button" href="/">Return to the app <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a> <button class="button secondary" data-print>Print source notes</button></div></article></Shell>
